WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.200
- [Mark] All right, Math, Math.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.033
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.384
Where are you from, originally?

00:00:07.384 --> 00:00:09.857
- [Math] I'm from La
Crescenta, California.

00:00:09.857 --> 00:00:12.607
It's at the top of the 2 freeway.

00:00:13.696 --> 00:00:15.696
- [Mark] A suburb of LA?

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:17.840
- Yeah.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.730
It's what's called a
census-designated place.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.650
They found that there was a place there

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.110
when they did the census.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.540
It's not part of a town or a city.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.710
It's part of the county unincorporated.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.980
But they didn't notice it
until they did the census.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.880
I read this on Wikipedia.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.730
- [Mark] And how was your childhood?

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.940
You had both your parents as a kid?

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.400
- I don't like the people who
raised me, either one of them.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.806
And I recently learned that I was adopted.

00:00:43.806 --> 00:00:45.263
So they're not my parents.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:50.745
I had a kind of miserable
time but it was a while ago.

00:00:50.745 --> 00:00:52.057
(chuckling) I had a kind of miserable time

00:00:52.057 --> 00:00:54.370
but it was a while ago, yeah.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:55.970
- [Mark] How would you
describe your childhood?

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.870
Was there abuse or anything like that?

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:02.135
- It was really boring.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:09.310
I wasn't allowed to go out
enough, have enough friends.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.300
I spent all my free time doing homework.

00:01:18.026 --> 00:01:20.980
Basically doing homework
and sometimes watching TV.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.980
They were very against me going out with

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.050
other kids my age and
partying or whatever.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.200
Very against drugs, very against dating,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.193
very against driving.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.220
So, I don't know, it sucked.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.631
- [Mark] So you went out
on your own at what age?

00:01:45.631 --> 00:01:48.910
- I lived there until I went to college

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:50.800
and I went to college when I was 17.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.163
I went to University
of Southern California.

00:01:53.163 --> 00:01:54.730
- You went to USC?
- Mm-hmm.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:55.871
Good for you.

00:01:55.871 --> 00:01:58.121
So you were a good student?

00:01:59.423 --> 00:02:02.538
- Yeah, I was a good student.

00:02:02.538 --> 00:02:05.563
- [Mark] And you went on
your own after school?

00:02:05.563 --> 00:02:07.483
- Went on my own after school.

00:02:10.326 --> 00:02:11.317
What do you mean?

00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:13.140
- [Mark] What did you
do after you graduated?

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.140
- After I graduated, then I
got a master's in New York.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.793
And then I,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.000
what did I do after?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.620
I learned to dance, finally.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.940
I stopped talking to them,
the people that raised me.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.343
I learned to do drugs, learned to dance,

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.840
learned to date, sort of.

00:02:42.327 --> 00:02:44.077
Had a long relationship with a boy.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.600
With a guy.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:49.910
His name is Steven.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:50.970
Oh, yeah, we had a band together.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:51.953
It was called Belt.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.520
- [Mark] Guys are your preference?

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.120
- Guys are my preference.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.940
I'm into, do you know
who David Griffith is?

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.620
I'm really into him.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.250
He's in Driving Miss Daisy.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.943
I think he calls himself a man, not a guy.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.103
What does he call himself?

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:12.743
An actor?

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.630
Oh, yeah, a really old drunk guy.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.350
Okay, he calls himself a guy, nevermind.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.703
Yeah, guys are my preference.

00:03:22.174 --> 00:03:25.073
I'm not into girls sexually
but I like girls, of course.

00:03:25.952 --> 00:03:27.253
- [Mark] And you were born a female?

00:03:27.253 --> 00:03:28.723
- Mm-hmm, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.090
You've worked, you've had jobs?

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.220
- Oh, yeah, I've had jobs.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:39.220
I was a host at a Sushi
and Shabu shabu restaurant.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:45.800
I was a editor of other people's

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.500
graduate school admissions essays.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:49.303
I was a

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.730
retail employee at American Apparel.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.730
And I was a person who let
you in, front desk person,

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.453
at a bath house in San
Francisco called Eros.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.120
- [Mark] So drugs came
into your life at what age?

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.520
- Drugs came into my life at what age.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.850
I think I was pretty young
the first time I had a drug.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.390
But I didn't start to do drugs

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.563
until I was in college, basically.

00:04:25.578 --> 00:04:28.150
I wish I had been able to
do more of them earlier.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.940
But the people who raised me,

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.340
my mom and dad and also my sister

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.440
are all very

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:42.930
scared of drugs.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.530
Although, they do them, kind of.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.640
Kind of, not really, but kind of.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:49.950
Me, I love drugs.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.070
- [Mark] What is your drug?

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.313
- My favorites are methamphetamine,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.670
cannabis, marijuana, and LSD.

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.600
I also really MDA and MDMA.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.953
- [Mark] And how do you
support yourself now?

00:05:15.977 --> 00:05:18.370
I've heard everything so
you can tell me whatever.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.530
- I want to make a feature film.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.970
I think Disney, the studio,

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.030
is going to help me make a feature film.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.340
Other things I know how
to do are be a cashier

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:31.570
and be the singer in a band

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:37.520
and go to the EBT office.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.413
- [Mark] Where are you staying now?

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.793
- Right now, I'm staying,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.110
I'm not sure the address, actually.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:52.559
- [Mark] But you're on the street?

00:05:52.559 --> 00:05:55.090
- Have been for almost two years.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.380
- [Mark] Two years.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:01.313
And how is that experience,
how has that been for you?

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.123
- Shitty, of course, boring, mostly.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.920
I've had a lot of trouble accessing

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.313
accurate information about
basic things involving housing.

00:06:16.827 --> 00:06:19.535
A housing office that isn't closed.

00:06:19.535 --> 00:06:23.020
Meeting a realtor that
actually gives you the place

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:26.693
to live and doesn't themselves,
somehow, use it instead.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.640
Partly because one of
the people who raised me

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:36.360
was a realtor who was either trained wrong

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:37.883
or unethical, or whatever.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.950
That's part of the reason
I have that problem.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.280
I'm in the wrong tradition of it.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.380
I'm in the misinformation tradition of it.

00:06:49.692 --> 00:06:54.580
Also, I've noticed this is a
huge problem for many people

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:59.260
in America, accessing
information about housing.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.390
I'm sure you've noticed a
lot of people live in tents

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:02.740
and probably don't want to.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.030
Sorry, (chuckling) I know that's boring.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.110
- [Mark] That's all right.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.460
You still have contact
with your family, or no?

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.410
- I don't consider my family
the people that raised me.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.130
And I don't want to be in touch with them.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.183
And mostly, I'm not.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:17.960
One of them recently filed

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.780
a missing person's report about me.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:21.870
And I think I was able to
address it and let them know

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.100
that I'm alive and that I
don't have to talk to them.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.780
Nobody has to like anybody, et cetera.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.010
- [Mark] How old are you now?

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:28.843
- I'm 40.

00:07:28.843 --> 00:07:30.020
I turned 40 in June.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.591
- [Mark] You're 40?

00:07:31.591 --> 00:07:33.300
Have you been in love before?

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.827
- [Math] Yeah, I've been
in love before, yeah.

00:07:35.827 --> 00:07:37.193
Have you?
- I have.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:43.133
Great experience until it's not.

00:07:44.361 --> 00:07:46.983
- [Math] (chuckling) Great
experience until it's not.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:51.443
- [Mark] But you've
had good relationships?

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.363
- [Math] Um, good relationships, yeah.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.130
Since I've been homeless, I
haven't had a phone or laptop,

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:11.130
in almost two years, to
express myself to people.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.350
Although, of course,
there's always talking.

00:08:20.006 --> 00:08:23.090
I'm like, oh, I want
to check and make sure

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:26.060
my conversations are good but I think so.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:29.500
I think I've had some really, really good

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.110
and deep relationships, not necessarily

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:35.536
that I even liked all of those people.

00:08:35.536 --> 00:08:37.086
- Do you have friends?
- Yeah.

00:08:41.777 --> 00:08:42.770
I have friends.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:44.460
I need to meet more of them in person.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.690
But I've done this type of thing before.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.390
Find someone really cool on Facebook

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:52.120
and buy them a plane
ticket to come visit me

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:53.920
and befriend them in person.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.920
And then say, okay, well,
see you again, sometime.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.880
I wish I was in a situation where I saw

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.863
my friends more often
or more on the computer.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:14.200
More on the computer
and then also in person.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.893
But I have friends, yeah.

00:09:19.579 --> 00:09:21.290
- [Mark] Do you have emotions

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.150
that you've struggled with
more often than others?

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.800
Depression, or anxiety, or
anger, anything like that?

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:32.160
- I have struggle expressing
myself emotionally,

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.260
or getting emotional,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.810
especially without having a place to stay.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:40.070
It's hard to break down
on the bus, or whatever,

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.857
really let go on the bus.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:16.400
Sometimes I'm too much of a
show off for the computer.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:17.853
If that makes sense.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.630
There's an artificial intelligence.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.080
Not an artificial intelligence, sorry,

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.350
but there's an intelligence
related to technology

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:28.630
or the computer and I'm like,

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:30.660
blah, blah, blah, blah,
blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.150
Check me out, check me out.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.960
Like, hi, camera, I'm saying
hi to you, the camera,

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.460
separately from saying
hi to the photographer

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:39.240
and separately from
saying to everything else.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.223
Check me out.

00:10:45.228 --> 00:10:48.050
And so I'm expressing
something to the camera

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:50.770
and ignoring the person.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:53.983
So whatever kind of emotion that is.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.960
There must be a word for this, I guess.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.780
Maybe I have a syndrome

00:11:12.059 --> 00:11:14.740
that FUCO doesn't know about.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:17.843
- [Mark] Have you been treated
for mental conditions before?

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:27.353
- Yeah, I love drugs.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.450
- [Mark] What are you
proudest of in your life?

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.333
- How good I am at naming
things, titling things.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:48.570
My favorite memory is
something with the person

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:50.320
who lived across the street from me

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.380
when I was growing up on
Raymond Ave in La Crescenta,

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.460
something with this
person, David Griffith.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:12:00.393
I'm not sure which part
of which part I mean.

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.290
One time, I got to sleep over at his house

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:06.840
and I was waking up in his house

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.070
and I was disoriented for a minute.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:09.903
Where am I?

00:12:09.903 --> 00:12:11.630
Oh, yeah, I'm in that guy's house.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:15.683
Oh, yeah, yeah, wow,
cool, I'm in his house.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:17.363
Maybe that.

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:21.483
And yeah, I'm really
good at titling things.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:27.320
One of my favorite things that
I've done in naming things

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:29.470
is I have a song called Limelight.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:32.770
And it's about Simba and
Nala from the Lion King.

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:35.520
And also it's about two phones
that got stolen from me.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:38.913
- [Mark] How did you end up homeless?

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:44.760
- I stopped being, it's
complicated and kind of boring.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.550
They weren't supposed to
be charging rent at all,

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:49.110
the people who were charging
rent, it wasn't their house.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:51.763
I stopped being able to
afford it and I was evicted.

00:12:54.987 --> 00:12:56.600
- [Mark] What's the most-important lesson

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.300
you've learned in your life?

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:01.050
- The most important lesson
I've learned in my life is,

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:27.060
the zodiac, the sign, gemini,
cancer, leo, et cetera,

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:28.700
is real, actually.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.200
For a long time, I dismissed it as,

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:35.020
or not dismissed it exactly
but didn't care about it

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:36.540
and it's really important

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:42.520
to understanding people

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:45.680
and understanding

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:55.373
animals, I guess.

00:13:57.190 --> 00:13:59.290
The signs are named after animals, mostly.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.170
Understanding sexuality, I don't think so.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:05.000
Really important to understanding people

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.553
and understanding racing, or something.

00:14:12.408 --> 00:14:13.241
- [Mark] Have you ever asked your parents

00:14:13.241 --> 00:14:14.800
how you got the name Math?

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.200
- They didn't name me that.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.150
When I was growing up,

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.560
my name was Laura Snyder,
Laura Holly Snyder.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:23.487
And I changed my name
to Math in 2001 or 2002.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:28.420
And then, in 2013, I made it legal.

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:31.110
I went to a courthouse in
Oakland and changed it there

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:33.760
and got it on my ID and stuff.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.953
- [Mark] Yeah, I saw it on your ID.

00:14:36.876 --> 00:14:38.613
Why did you choose the name Math?

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.023
- Math tenure means the Big Dipper.

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:54.790
Sorry (chuckling).

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:56.950
Why did I choose the name Math.

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:58.050
I'm good with numbers.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:00.843
I wanted something more androgynous.

00:15:02.872 --> 00:15:07.872
I got this idea to have a dog
named Math or a cat named Math

00:15:08.868 --> 00:15:13.130
because it's such a cool idea
to have a pet named Math.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:14.620
- [Mark] Yeah, Math is
an interesting name.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:17.500
- There's Math running around
in your kitchen, or whatever.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:19.940
Math wants something.

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:22.753
And I thought about it for
a little while and decided,

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:26.920
yeah, I want it as my own
name, even though it's cooler

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.917
if it's a cat or a dog's name,
I like it as my own name.

00:15:30.917 --> 00:15:33.390
And I don't really have pets.

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.533
- [Mark] Your androgyny is
something that you play up?

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:43.703
- Oh, sorry.

00:15:50.203 --> 00:15:52.003
I'll try not to do that (chuckling).

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.047
- [Mark] Your androgyny is
something that you play up?

00:16:02.704 --> 00:16:06.537
- I'm having trouble
thinking of the reference

00:16:09.564 --> 00:16:12.073
but I love David Bowie.

00:16:12.073 --> 00:16:15.823
That's a quote from a
Bowie interview, right?

00:16:17.593 --> 00:16:19.172
- [Mark] I'm just asking you a question.

00:16:19.172 --> 00:16:21.620
- My androgyny is something
that I play up, or--

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:23.373
- Cultivate?
- Cultivate.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:26.240
- [Mark] Comes to you naturally?

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:28.020
- Me, I don't think about it that much.

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:29.973
My pronoun is he, but whatever.

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:32.350
Oh, sorry, my pronoun is he,

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:34.123
but it's not a big deal, I swear.

00:16:37.295 --> 00:16:39.283
Androgyny is something I play up.

00:16:48.489 --> 00:16:50.020
I don't want to get defensive.

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:51.350
I assume you're letting me know

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:55.040
that it is something I play up even though

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.450
it isn't something I thought I played up.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:00.610
- [Mark] Well, you have
a beard and sideburns.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:01.970
- Oh, yeah, I do take testosterone.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:05.793
It's been a while since
I had a fresh vial.

00:17:07.107 --> 00:17:08.307
Oh, I see what you mean.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:12.406
Yes.

00:17:12.406 --> 00:17:16.623
- [Mark] (chuckling)
Okay, we got an answer.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:19.613
- I take testosterone.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:23.580
- [Mark] Do you prefer being,

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:27.233
would you prefer to be considered a male?

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:31.270
- I get a huge thrill
out of being called he.

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:33.747
But also, I--

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:36.210
- When I met you, it was unclear

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:38.640
whether you were male or female.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.559
- Yeah, I'm not trying to do that

00:17:41.559 --> 00:17:45.663
but I don't want to
think about it that much.

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:53.390
How someone sees me, it's
fine if they see me as a girl.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:55.040
Of course, it's fine (chuckling).

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:00.660
I get a huge thrill out of
it when they see me as a guy.

00:18:01.661 --> 00:18:03.280
Am I ever really trying to do something

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:06.210
where it's confusing
if I'm a girl or a guy?

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:07.773
I guess.

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:12.190
Anyway, I don't mind
when people ask about it.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:15.650
- [Mark] We're all free
to be who we should be.

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:17.140
Who we wanna be, right?

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:18.633
- Yeah, (chuckling) yeah.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.510
Sometimes I say that being
a girl is the worst thing

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:24.230
I can think of and I'm exaggerating.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.770
It's not, well, it is the worst thing

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:28.833
I can think of, for some reason.

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:31.800
It's the worst thing
I want to think about.

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.390
I don't want to think about murder, or--

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:36.810
- [Mark] You think being a woman is--

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:38.360
- Well, I'm sort of joking.

00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:42.120
If you're a girl and you're a
girl and you like being a girl

00:18:44.061 --> 00:18:47.590
then I don't have any
idea what that's like.

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:53.490
Being a girl, I say this all
the time, sort of as a joke,

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:56.120
being a girl is the worst
thing I can think of.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:57.604
It's not the worst thing I can think of

00:18:57.604 --> 00:19:00.354
but it's the worst thing I
can comfortably think about.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:03.960
- [Mark] Are you happy?

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.230
Would you describe yourself as happy?

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.430
- I'm in a good mood
almost all the time but

00:19:14.044 --> 00:19:16.500
I'm kind of miserable.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:19.000
I need a computer and a
phone and a place to live.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.193
But I'll have them soon.

00:19:20.193 --> 00:19:23.210
I'm in a pretty good
mood almost all the time.

00:19:25.933 --> 00:19:27.510
Always have been.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:33.313
I don't get depressed hardly ever.

00:19:36.632 --> 00:19:37.465
- [Mark] That's a good thing.

00:19:37.465 --> 00:19:42.023
- Yeah, so only comparatively.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:48.320
Depressed compared to how I
was last year, or whatever.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:53.320
But compared to people who
actually have depressions,

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:56.450
I don't really get those.

00:19:56.450 --> 00:19:57.283
I have

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:13.140
extremely good mental health or I have a

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:16.670
syndrome that hasn't been named yet

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:21.150
that has to do with trying
to impress the cartoon,

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:28.080
or whatever, more than I try
to connect with a person.

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:35.457
- [Mark] Okay (chuckling).

00:20:37.284 --> 00:20:39.637
All right, Math, thank you
very much for talking with me.

00:20:39.637 --> 00:20:41.290
And I wish you best of luck out there.

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:42.123
Be careful.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:44.590
- Oh, yeah.

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:46.650
- [Mark] The streets are dangerous.

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:48.100
- Yeah, I know what you mean.

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:51.547
- [Mark] All right, thank you very much.

00:20:51.547 --> 00:20:54.638
- You're welcome.

00:20:54.638 --> 00:20:57.388
- You're interesting.
- Thank you.

